<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahendradatta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">10th–11th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Gunapriyadharmapatni</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahendradatta was a Javanese princess of the Isyana dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a> who married King Udayana of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> in the late 10th century and ruled jointly with him. Their son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/airlangga">Airlangga</a> became king of Kahuripan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, and another son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anak-wungsu">Anak Wungsu</a>, later ruled Bali. Her marriage strengthened Javanese cultural influence on the Balinese court.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dharmawangsa, who had moved the capital to Wwatan near Madiun and married his sister <b>Mahendradatta</b> to Udayana Warmadewa of Bali, invaded Srivijaya by sea in 990 to take Palembang; the Maharaja Sri Cudamani Warmadewa won Chinese support and repelled the invasion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>King Udayana married the Javanese princess <b>Mahendradatta</b>, and their son Airlangga later ruled Kahuripan in Java.</blockquote><a href="/state/bedulu-kingdom">Bedulu Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
